  "body": "# CoAkka Runtime 2.1.0\n\nRelease identity:\n\n- runtime version: `2.1.0`\n- native generation: `2.1.0+60ddf70d`\n- full native source commit: `60ddf70d63b94750bb76c8284923e73199788c2e`\n- connector source commit: `4782dcd`\n- JVM, Spring Boot, and Quarkus coordinate:\n  `2.1.0-g60ddf70d-4782dcd`\n- source connector artifact generation:\n  `2.1.0+60ddf70d-4782dcd`\n\n## File Lane\n\nRuntime 2.1.0 adds the independent file lane: a bounded point-to-point path\nfor transferring one immutable file from Service A to Service B after Service\nB authorizes and prepares the exact transfer identity.\n\nThe application control plane carries the transfer ID, a cryptographically\nstrong single-use token, expected byte count, SHA-256 digest, receiver host,\nand receiver port. File bytes do not travel inside a runtime `Envelope`.\nService B writes a staging file, checkpoints durable progress, verifies the\ncomplete digest, and publishes the destination only after verification.\n\nDirect mode uses the operating-system file-transfer facility available on the\nhost: Linux/macOS `sendfile` and Windows `TransmitFile`. This describes the\nfile-to-socket portion of the send path. The complete transfer is not an\nend-to-end zero-copy claim: the receiver still writes storage, verifies the\nfile, and performs an atomic publish boundary. TLS and mTLS intentionally use\nthe encrypted streaming path because encryption changes the ownership and\ncopy boundary.\n\nUse the file lane for large media, models, datasets, backup/checkpoint files,\nbuild artifacts, or diagnostic bundles exchanged between trusted application\nservices. Use normal messages for commands and events. Use object storage,\nCDNs, or HTTPS when content needs broad distribution, caching, browser access,\nor long-lived addressing.\n\nThe full workflow, JVM/JNA/JNI bridge explanation, Service A and Service B\nexamples, authorization rules, lifecycle, cancellation, resume behavior, and\nresult vocabulary are documented in\n[Runtime File Transfer](https://github.com/phuong-tran/coakka-publish/blob/main/docs/runtime-file-transfer.md).\n\n## Message And Failure Semantics\n\nThe common message documentation now defines the complete public `Envelope`,\nincluding `ONE_WAY`: it suppresses reply production but does not suppress\nadmission, routing, transport, observability, or deadletter behavior.\n\nThe deadletter map separates pre-admission rejection from failure after runtime\nowns an envelope. It also defines TLS/mTLS behavior:\n\n- invalid local TLS configuration is returned synchronously and does not\n  create a deadletter;\n- an outbound security failure for an admitted envelope creates\n  `REMOTE_TRANSPORT_SECURITY_FAILED` with `transport-failure/1` metadata;\n- an inbound handshake failure before a frame is decoded has no envelope\n  identity and is recorded as a typed runtime event.\n\nSee [Envelope And Deadletter Map](https://github.com/phuong-tran/coakka-publish/blob/main/docs/envelope-deadletter-map.md) and\n[Runtime TLS And mTLS](https://github.com/phuong-tran/coakka-publish/blob/main/docs/tls-and-mtls.md).\n\n## Connector Surface\n\nThe file lane is available through the native C ABI and the JVM, Python,\nNode.js, Bun, Electron main process, Go, C#, Rust, Swift, Zig, Mojo, and trusted\nTauri host lanes. Electron renderers and Tauri WebViews receive narrow\napplication intents rather than raw paths, tokens, or native handles.\n\nSpring Boot and Quarkus consume the same documented JVM connector generation.\nThe JVM surface is the `JVM/JNA/JNI` native-bridge category; the current\nimplementation calls the C ABI through JNA.\n\n## Native Matrix And Runtime Evidence\n\nThe release contains all five required native libraries:\n\n| Platform | Library | Build and execution evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Linux ARM64 | `libcoakka_runtime_v2.so` | Host-native UTM build and file-lane roundtrip |\n| Linux x86-64 | `libcoakka_runtime_v2.so` | Host-native UTM build and file-lane roundtrip |\n| macOS ARM64 | `libcoakka_runtime_v2.dylib` | Host-native build and file-lane roundtrip |\n| Windows ARM64 | `libcoakka_runtime_v2.dll` | MSVC build; file-lane and 10 TLS/mTLS tests executed on Windows 11 ARM64 |\n| Windows x86-64 | `libcoakka_runtime_v2.dll` | MSVC build; file-lane and 10 TLS/mTLS tests executed under Windows 11 ARM64 x64 emulation |\n\nEvery native library exports the same 115-symbol C ABI and reports the full\nsource commit through runtime introspection. Release verification also checks\nbinary architecture, dependency policy, manifest identity, archive hashes,\nand extraction on matching hosts.\n\nThe Windows runtime gate now executes the file-lane roundtrip as part of the\nrelease build. That gate caught and fixed a real CRT file-position issue after\nsource SHA-256 verification; the final 9,437,915-byte Windows transfer reaches\n`COMPLETED + OK` independently on both sender and receiver.\n\n## Publication Boundary\n\nThe artifact mirror, Maven repository, Go module, SwiftPM package, package\narchives, native tools, and package-manager registries are separate release\nchannels. npm Node.js, Bun, and Electron `2.1.1` are published and\nclean-install verified. NuGet `CoAkka.Runtime==2.1.0` and PyPI\n`coakka-v2-connector==2.1.0` are also published and clean-install verified\nfrom connector sources `99bb16c` and `34d15e7`. The PyPI registry wheel\nmatches the staged candidate SHA-256 exactly.\n\nThe public source tags are `coakka-runtime-go@v1.5.1` and\n`coakka-runtime-swift@v2.1.1`. Both documentation-integrity patches retain\nnative generation `2.1.0+60ddf70d`. Go remains on semantic major `v1` because its\nestablished module path does not carry the `/v2` suffix.",
